Figure 4.6 shows the J/\(\psi\) reconstruction efficiencies as a function of \(p_{T}\) for all centrality classes. The efficiency is a strong function \(p_{T}\) and centrality. For all centrality classes, the efficiency appears to have a positive slope in the region of \(0<p_{T}<0.25\) GeV/c. Beyond that, the efficiency dips down until \(p_{T}\sim 1.5\) GeV/c, where again it raises and eventually reaches a plateau in the vicinity of \(p_{T}\sim 3.5\) GeV/c. The dip in the efficiency is induced by the \(dE/dx\) PID cuts, and the strong centrality dependence is induced by the \(nHitsDedx\) cuts, which also exhibit a strong centrality dependence.

